# What goes into the source distribution.
#
# setuptools' default sdist is just the package plus a few project files. That
# is not enough here: the README links to docs/*.md on nearly every claim, and
# an sdist consumer would find every one of those links broken. The same applies
# to `benchmarks/results.json`, which is the recorded evidence behind the
# measured numbers the README quotes.
#
# The wheel is unaffected — this governs the sdist only. The installed package
# is still `app/`, and none of this becomes a runtime dependency.

include LICENSE
include README.md
include SECURITY.md
include RELEASE_NOTES.md
include CHANGELOG.md
include Makefile
include .python-version

# Documentation the README links to, including the ADRs that record why the
# design decisions were made.
recursive-include docs *.md

# The benchmark suite and its recorded results. `results.json` is the evidence
# for every benchmark number quoted in the README; shipping it means a reader
# can check those numbers rather than take them on trust.
recursive-include benchmarks *.py *.json

# Fixture sources. The macro fixture is generated from `fixtures/vba.py`, so the
# source is required even though no binary is committed.
recursive-include fixtures *.py

# The test suite, so `make test` and the CI gates are reproducible from an
# sdist without fetching the repository.
recursive-include tests *.py

# Never ship local run output. Caches and bytecode are already excluded by
# setuptools, and listing them again only produces "no previously-included
# files matching" warnings on every build.
prune .excelpilot
prune .github
